What needed care in the translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **glibc [38;5;10mrand()[0m** — the C output depends entirely on the exact PRNG stream, so I reimplemented glibc's TYPE_3 additive-feedback [38;5;10mrandom_r[0m (31-word state, separation 3, Schrage seeding, 310 discarded outputs, [38;5;10m>> 1[0m on the [38;5;10mu32[0m sum). Includes the [38;5;10mseed == 0 → 1[0m fixup and the fact that seeds above [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m make [38;5;10mstate[0][0m negative.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mstrtoul[0m semantics** — reimplemented for base 10 with leading whitespace, [38;5;10m+[0m/[38;5;10m-[0m sign, wrapping negation, [38;5;10mendptr[0m-back-to-[38;5;10mnptr[0m on no digits, and [38;5;10mULONG_MAX[0m+ERANGE on overflow. This matters: [38;5;10m"-0"[0m and [38;5;10m"-18446744073709547521"[0m are [3maccepted[23m seeds in the C, and the validation order ([38;5;10m*endptr[0m → [38;5;10merrno[0m → [38;5;10m> UINT_MAX[0m) is preserved.[0m[0m
- **Signed overflow / shifts** — the hot loop relies on UB that gcc compiles as wrapping, so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_mul/add/sub/shl[0m, arithmetic [38;5;10m>>[0m, and Rust's C-compatible truncating [38;5;10m/[0m and [38;5;10m%[0m. Argv is handled as raw bytes so non-UTF-8 arguments echo identically.[0m[0m
